is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router4
diagnosis_text:
Location C (192.168.200.0/24) is isolated at its default gateway, router4. The affected component is Layer-3 routing/RIP on router4’s uplink interfaces. Hosts in Location C can reach each other, but any traffic to Location A (172.16.10.0/24, 172.16.20.0/24) is handed to router4 (192.168.200.1), which has no working upstream connections and immediately returns ICMP “Destination Net Unreachable.”

Mechanism: From pc13 to 172.16.10.4, replies are “From 192.168.200.1 Destination Net Unreachable,” proving the gateway is rejecting off-subnet traffic. On router4, the routing table has only the connected 192.168.200.0/24 (no RIP-learned routes), and both uplink interfaces eth4 (192.168.15.2/24 toward router5) and eth5 (172.25.1.1/24 toward router3) are DOWN (ip -br link shows eth4 DOWN, eth5 DOWN). A ping from router4 to its neighbor 172.25.1.2 fails (RC=2). Conversely, the rest of the network still has RIP routes to 192.168.200.0/24 via 172.25.1.1 (seen on router3), which explains why other zones interconnect normally but cannot actually reach Location C through the dead uplink.

Localization: router4, interfaces eth4 (to router5/192.168.15.0/24) and eth5 (to router3/172.25.1.0/24). Root cause: router4’s uplink interfaces are administratively down, preventing RIP exchange and upstream forwarding.

Corrective fix: On router4, bring up eth4 and eth5 (e.g., ip link set eth4 up; ip link set eth5 up), then verify RIP (matching networks 192.168.15.0/24 and 172.25.0.0/16) installs routes to 172.16.10.0/24 and 172.16.20.0/24. After links are up, Location C should regain connectivity to Location A. === END ===